# Digest 19: pattern 11, Potty readiness and learning

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests/share-19.md. Yes posts: 38. Near posts: 50.

## Pattern 11, share 19: yes posts

### They notice possible readiness signs or hear early-training claims and ask what those signs mean.

5 of 38 posts.

The children are 8, 13, 14, 15 and 20 months old. Parents mention dry sleep periods, hiding to poop, taking off wet diapers or being unable to spot urination cues; one asks how an influencer's reported infant toileting could work. They ask when and how to start rather than reporting that these signs establish readiness.

- "isn’t 13m a bit early to potty train how do i do this where do i start i thought i had another 6m- a year before i had to even think about this. advice is very needed" [sr046-0024]
- "My main issue is she is autistic and hasn't started talking, she nom verbally communicates but doesn't tell me when she goes in her diaper currently and I have no idea where I would even start." [sr001-0014]
- "I have no idea when my 20 month old is peeing - I haven't been able to pick up on any signs." [sr012-0150]
- "Curious if anyone has any thoughts or experience on Elimination communication? Is it too late to start? Have also read that it’s best to start potty training between 18-30 months before the “no” phase happens and that EC makes babies do something they’re not developmentally ready for yet." [sr076-0081]
- "If there is please tell me bc my baby is 15 months and I would like to stop fighting with him to change his diaper." [sr028-0131]

**But:** The parent reporting autism corrects that description: "That was supposed to say suspected autistic oops" [sr001-0016]. A dry diaper or aversion to changes is not reported here as proof that the child can use the potty.

Posts: 1s9cj23 1sqioze 1tbhss5 1tzwj53 1v8yonj

### They want to introduce the potty gently before committing to full training.

7 of 38 posts.

These parents describe children from 8 to 22 months, including a child turning two in November. They ask about brief sits, following adults into the bathroom, responding to sounds or diaper-change signals, and fitting practice around daycare or bath time. Some have only bought or considered a potty; others already offer it and want help with comfort or the next step.

- "So yeah I wasn't thinking about training in the sense that it needs to be done ASAP and I want to take her out of diapers. I just want to see if she wants to go on the potty. If she's potty trained in 2 weeks great, if it takes 2 years, also great. I'm not anticipating any mess or stress because I definitely don't want to introduce underwear yet. Just maybe pull ups." [sr058-0067]
- "My thought at this point is just to practice having her sit on a potty at home a few times a day, no pressure, to familiarize her with the routine, but since shes not showing most of the readiness signs, I'm worried this is a bad idea? Would love input from parents who have gone through potty training." [sr019-0130]
- "What’s the best way to introduce it? I was thinking of just putting it right next to the toilet in his bathroom and when I get up to go to the bathroom in the morning setting him on his potty to start building that association?" [sr057-0028]
- "Any tips or tricks on how to get him comfortable or used to it? Or EC tips in general? I’m not expecting a lot or trying to be super hardcore with it, just being very relaxed but proactive when it comes to potty training." [sr048-0083]
- "Where do we go from here? Potty training is something I feel the least prepared for! I've simply been going on vibes." [sr058-0030]
- "Would it make sense for me to start an elimination communication style and put her on the toilet before the bath every night?" [sr048-0014]

**But:** Interest does not always lead to comfort or timely elimination: "I recently bought the Baby Bjorn potty seat and he’s really been struggling with it." [sr048-0082].

Posts: 1swbw60 1tzvieq 1u3w3ne 1ue315b 1uesojn 1uf6qrn 1vdl60h

### They question other people's instructions about the age to begin potty learning.

2 of 38 posts.

One first-time parent follows a 15-month-old's interest while relatives say this forces growing up too soon. Another says a health visitor advised starting at 11 months and asks whether other parents received the same information. The reported advice pushes in opposite directions; this digest records the accounts without checking the guidance.

- "My son (15 months) has recently started to become very interested in the bathroom, particularly the toilet. We didn’t plan to attempt any kind of potty training until about 18 months but I have started to lean into his interest and my family says that I’m forcing him to grow up too fast." [sr021-0011]
- "All of this has really been weighing on my mind, this is our first child and we’re learning as we go but our family made us feel really shitty about even thinking of attempting." [sr021-0014]
- "The nhs guidance has recently changed in terms of when to start potty training. My HV gave me a call this morning to go over it and she said that we should start potty training our 11 month old and aim for her to be out of nappies by 18 months - 2 years. I’m just wondering if anyone else has received this info from their HV." [sr103-0210]
- "And if anyone has any suggestions on how to make this a bit easier" [sr103-0211]

**But:** The parent following early interest accepts an uncertain timetable: "We aren’t on any strict timeline to have him potty trained, ideally we’d like him to be day trained by 2 but we’ll take it as it comes." [sr021-0017].

Posts: 1szf2xp 1w9o6rd

### They question whether a training method can fit outings and interruptions.

2 of 38 posts.

Both children are approaching two: one is 22 months and the other turns two the following month. A vacation and long drive make prolonged commando training feel impractical in one account; last-minute outings and daycare diaper changes interrupt the other family's attempt. The parents ask whether to keep going with a modified routine or wait for uninterrupted time.

- "Namely, what do you mean they need to be commando for “weeks”?" [sr009-0033]
- "Here’s my other issue. We have a long weekend vacation planned for the end of this month. We will be at the beach, we will be out and about doing activities. A bathroom will not be quickly accessible to us at all times. It’s also a 5 hour road trip there and back. I don’t know how this meshes with the potty training plan." [sr009-0034]
- "I’m not sure if I should proceed and try to keep training at home and school, or wait for a few weeks until I have a full weekend to really try no underwear with no diaper breaks?" [sr016-0070]
- "After my post, my toddler went back to her Montessori daycare on Monday, I brought training underwear, wet bags, and explained how things went during the weekend. At pickup, she was wearing diapers, they told us they offered the toilet, she did not show enough interest apparently, and they just put her back in diapers. I am still trying at home but it is definitely going slower than with my first." [sr016-0079]

**But:** The families describe ways they might continue: "We are doing 3 days of no bottoms and then I was going to switch to the undies." [sr009-0036].

Posts: 1sgxp2p 1swhi96

### They find that childcare rules or facilities conflict with the potty routine they want.

3 of 38 posts.

The children are 19 months, 22 months and nearly two. One classroom will not offer the potty, another insists on training underwear, and a classroom move brings a larger toilet and less adult help after earlier progress. Parents weigh changing rooms, waiting, speaking to school or adapting equipment.

- "However, her daycare classroom does not take kids to the potty. She'll be attending a different daycare starting at the end of July." [sr025-0160]
- "I’m a new parent to Montessori education, my 19m old son just started in pre-casa and they have insisted on no diapers or pull-ups, training undies only. Is this the norm?" [sr107-0032]
- "The problem started about two weeks ago when he moved daycare classrooms to the next class up." [sr045-0072]
- "I asked if I could bring a smaller potty seat they could easily put on and take off, which they were happy to do. However, even though the toilet seat is larger than the previous classroom's, it's not a standard size. I've tried several different potty seats, and they're all too big to fit on the classroom's toilet seat. The teacher that's in his classroom in the morning is very understanding and patient, but the teacher that's there in the afternoon seems overwhelmed and often sends him home in a diaper." [sr045-0074]
- "We are trying our best and attempting to be consistent at home but this has resulted in a lot of accidents. He knows the sign for toilet and will sit on the potty but he rarely does anything. In my opinion, I feel like it might be too early for him and he’s just not ready yet to recognize the connection between the feeling of needing to go with going to the potty." [sr107-0034]

**But:** Childcare is not uniformly unsupportive: "Daycare was supportive and said they were happy to follow whatever we did at home" [sr045-0070]. The same parent describes a later classroom-specific setback.

Posts: 1t5dquv 1ttrehq 1wejwbj

### They weigh potty training against a new sibling and the attention other transitions require.

4 of 38 posts.

These accounts include an 18-month-old, a child nearly one, a 22-month-old with a two-month-old sibling, and a child whose age is unknown. Parents ask whether to train before birth, after adjustment or alongside newborn care. Bed transitions, weaning, diaper leaks and uncertain willingness add to the timing decision.

- "Few issues though, he hates sitting on the potty (whether it’s the big one or his little one) and will cry if I sit him on it. He also doesn’t seem to know when he pees at all. Also, I’m due with baby 2 next month and I heard it’s a bad idea to potty train right before a new sibling comes. So should I wait until he’s closer to like 22 months and adjusted to having his sister around? Or do it now before she comes?" [sr019-0113]
- "Wondering as I think these things happen around 2 years and am wondering if the transition would be smoother before or after baby. Or any advice in general. Thanks!" [sr027-0219]
- "The potty training seems like the obvious hurtle to tackle last but it also seems important to do before the new baby comes and my attention is split." [sr110-0051]
- "I feel like now a the time but I’m worried because he’s also showing me some signs of defiance (like screaming or saying no when I ask him to do something) which is normal for his age but wondering how that factors into the teaching. I’m also worried about how I pay attention to him 100% while also taking care of my 2 month old." [sr122-0029]

**But:** Waiting also has costs in one account: "Honestly I’d put off the training longer for convenience but he is consistently peeing through diapers despite sizing up and changing him frequently and has shown me all the readiness signs for toilet training." [sr122-0028].

