## Pattern 11, share 19: near posts

### They notice possible readiness signs or hear early-training claims and ask what those signs mean.

5 of 50 posts.

One parent asks whether a nonwalking 10–11-month-old can train; another is reconsidering after an unsuccessful attempt the previous year. Other posts ask starting and completion ages, contrast early sibling training with reports of starting at three or four, or ask about progress as school approaches. The remaining children's ages are unknown here, apart from the two-year-old described in the share's account of the renewed attempt.

- "Is it possible to potty train a 10-11 month old that isn’t walking yet ? How does this work?" [sr036-0045]
- "Hey guys! I feel like my toddler is almost ready for potty training. For context, I did try last year and felt she wasn’t ready for it, so decided to wait it out. Do you have any advice or tips that work well for toddlers? She now knows what potty means and lets me know when she does it in her diaper. She also loves to sit on the potty box with her diaper on 🤣🫠 any advice would be appreciated!!  
Thanks in advance 😊" [sr023-0030]
- "Hi all! I am mostly just curious - when did everyone start potty training their littles? My oldest kid was fully day trained by 20 months and night trained completely before she turned 3. Once my little one starts walking confidently I plan on introducing the idea to her, but I keep seeing stuff about people starting at 3-4!" [sr064-0071]
- "I wanted to know- How old was your toddler when you started potty training - but then how old were they when they were actually potty trained?" [sr067-0070]
- "School starts in a few weeks here. The final countdown has begun. Has your LO made the leap and can go potty by themselves, in the throes of a last minute potty training marathon, or it is destined for failure?" [sr090-0073]

**But:** The early-trained older sibling had a specific institutional deadline: "With my oldest, she had to be fully trained by 2 so she could go to a specific daycare" [sr064-0072]. That account does not describe an unconstrained choice of age.

Posts: 1t10th1 1tn8g6d 1upmjvl 1uwe84x 1vl3mmw

### They ask for a workable way to start or motivate potty use.

5 of 50 posts.

These posts ask where to start, what has worked, how to motivate a child and how to train without nakedness or urine throughout the house. One parent of a two-year-old asks about underwear, pull-ups, wiping and carpet; the other ages are unknown. One account also describes designing keepsakes and printables, so its preparation narrative sits alongside self-reported resource-making.

- "I honestly don't even know where to start with potty training." [sr012-0033]
- "Need help to train my kid for potty,. How to motivate her? I think she knows when she wants to go but didn't go.  
I tried rewards but didn't work.  
Is there any science based solutions for motivation?" [sr020-0046]
- "Just want to know what worked. What didn't. What the bad ideas were." [sr050-0004]
- "Hi all! I’m looking for advice on how to potty train my toddler without going full naked. He’s not comfortable being naked, and I’m not comfortable with the idea of pee getting all over my house! So I’d like to train using pull-ups and/ or underwear. Any advice would be super appreciated!" [sr070-0111]
- "I'm one of those parents who likes to be prepared, so I've already started putting together a simple potty training chart ready for when she's showing all the signs she's ready. I wanted something fun and encouraging that celebrates the little wins rather than focusing on accidents or setbacks." [sr037-0008]

**But:** Some preparation has already happened: "I have gotten one of those tiny toilets that makes the flushing sounds." [sr012-0033]. Wanting a method does not always mean having done nothing.

