### They want to respond to potty requests without letting them take over the day.

2 of 50 posts.

A two-year-old sits for books or videos without producing anything and uses requests to delay transitions, according to the parent. In the other account, a child of unknown age repeatedly leaves bed for pee or poop, extending bedtime. The parents want manageable limits without adding pressure or refusing a genuine bodily need.

- "He’s also learned to strategically ask to potty when it’s time to go somewhere, put shoes on, eat dinner, or take a bath or go to bed." [sr039-0032]
- "He’s our first kid and we need a low-pressure, low-effort approach that is feasible with 2 working parents and a newborn sibling." [sr039-0036]
- "BUT THEN! This child draggggs this part out for another half hour. She clings to me and jumps out of bed crying saying she has to pee. She always does actually pee, but she refuses to go right before bed. Usually she will get back in bed, and then I will tuck her in and then she will suddenly cling and cry and say NOOOO I HAVE TO GO POOOOP!!! About 30% of the time, the child does poop, so I feel like I can’t force her to stay in bed. When she gets out to poop, I set the timer for 5 minutes and she has to go in that time or she goes back to bed." [sr102-0050]
- "Anyway, it makes bedtime maddening and I wish she would just go before bed, but I can’t do the thing where I tell her she has no other chances because I also can’t let her pee or poop her pull-up. Anyone deal with something similar? Help 😅" [sr102-0051]

**But:** The bedtime trips are not all empty delaying tactics: "She always does actually pee" [sr102-0050].

