With ages unknown, these parents describe pee on couches or floors, crying or refusal on the potty, and repeated reminders that still miss the moment. They have tried combinations of timed sits, rewards, phone distraction, songs, breaks and catching a bowel movement in progress. They ask whether the child is too young, whether to continue and how to avoid making the experience frightening.

- "I need some reassurance here. We started the Oh Crap potty training method yesterday and so far there’s been minimal pee in the potty, so much pee on the floor and couch (I might need to burn this couch when we’re done) and three poops that miraculously made it in the potty (she cried out and I grabbed her a plopped her down to finish pooping on the potty). When she pees she looks confused and says “uh oh I pee” Or something to that effect." [sr040-0036]
- "When she pees on the floor I am expressing frustration and asking for help cleaning it up. My husband seems to think my frustration is much scarier than I mean it to be. This is a larger issue in my life so he's probably right. But like, Jesus. It's been weeks, she only pees on the floor, she screams bloody murder at the gentlest redirections to the potty. Genuinely how am I supposed to convey that she should stop peeing on the floor, if I cannot express that it frustrates mama?" [sr123-0025]
- "We get up, change diaper, get into undies. I set a pee timer for every 20 minutes. We sit on the potty every single time. After three 20 minutes timers I decide we should probably shorten the time frame EXTREMELY. We start going in 3 minute intervals, my child is up and ready going every time. It’s been 2 hrs, they still haven’t went, I change the timer to 1 minute. Timer goes off, we run to the potty, my child decides they need a bath toy on their way by, as they are grabbing it they pee onto the floor." [sr125-0093]
- "We’ve been in a very similar routine for the past 2 days. I’m feeling frustrated because we are RIGHT there at the potty and my child pees onto the floor. I don’t want to become an angry parent, and I don’t want to FORCE them onto the potty I want it to be a good experience and not a traumatizing one." [sr125-0094]
- "No I know, I was trying to illustrate a larger point that the issue is sitting still. She announces when she needs to peepee, sits on it for literally 2 seconds, and gets up. Anything I do to try to extend that time is such a big meltdown I genuinely feel like I am abusing her. I say "thanks for trying" and "if you pee in the potty and you can have candy" and this has been going on for weeks without change." [sr123-0032]

